<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/advanced/climate-modelling-and-scenarios">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Climate models are <strong>the most physically-constrained simulations ever built</strong> —
    the same fluid dynamics that forecasts weather, coupled to oceans, ice and carbon, run
    forward for centuries under scenarios humans choose. They are tested against the past
    continuously, and their uncertainty bands are honest work, not hedging. The syllabus's
    atmosphere-and-human-activities unit (4.1–4.4) supplies the pieces — atmosphere structure,
    the enhanced greenhouse effect, acid rain, ozone — and this lesson assembles the machine
    that turns that physics into numbers a policy-maker can argue with: how the models are
    built, what they can and cannot resolve, how scenarios are defined, how the models are
    validated, and how professionals read a projection.
  </p>
  <p>
    The weather-versus-climate distinction is the first skill. A weather model predicts
    the <em>state</em> of the atmosphere on a given day, and chaos caps its horizon at
    ~two weeks (the differential-equations lesson's sensitive dependence). A climate
    model predicts the <em>statistics</em> — the distribution of weather — which chaos
    does not prevent, any more than casino unpredictability prevents the house knowing
    its long-run edge. Climate models therefore answer a different, gentler question
    ("if the blanket thickens this much, where does the energy balance settle?") with
    the same equations.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the physics, the grids, the scenarios, the validation" widget="equationStep" config={modelCfg} />

  <h2>Anatomy of a climate model</h2>
  <p>
    The core is a <strong>general circulation model</strong>: Earth divided into a
    three-dimensional grid (~100 km horizontally, ~30 vertical layers in atmosphere and
    ocean), and on each cell the conservation laws — energy, mass, momentum, moisture —
    stepped forward in minutes of simulated time (the same numerics as the Euler and
    Runge–Kutta schemes of the differential-equations lesson, run on millions of
    processors). Around the atmosphere wrap the couplings: an <strong>ocean model</strong>
    (which stores 90% of the extra heat — the Argo floats that measure it are the
    model's audit trail), sea ice, land surface and vegetation, and — in full
    <strong>Earth system models</strong> (ESMs) — the interactive carbon and nitrogen
    cycles of the geochemical-cycles lesson, so the sinks' future behaviour is computed
    rather than prescribed. The unavoidable compromise is <strong>parameterisation</strong>:
    processes smaller than the grid — individual cumulus clouds, turbulence, aerosol
    chemistry — must be represented by statistical recipes calibrated on observations
    and small-scale models. Clouds are the hardest (they both cool, by reflecting
    sunlight, and warm, by trapping radiation) and are the dominant reason model
    sensitivities disagree — the honest head of every uncertainty bar.
  </p>

  <h2>The physics spine: forcing, feedback, sensitivity</h2>
  <p>
    Beneath the supercomputers sits arithmetic you can do on paper, and it organises
    everything:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the forcing–feedback–sensitivity chain — the calculation every projection is built on" widget="equationStep" config={ecsCfg} />

  <p>
    Two features of that chain deserve emphasis. The <strong>logarithm</strong> is
    physically real (CO₂'s absorption bands saturate at the centre, so the edges keep
    adding), and it means the marginal effect of a tonne falls as concentrations rise —
    the physics behind the "carbon budget" framing being in cumulative tonnes rather
    than percentages. And the <strong>feedback inventory</strong> is where models earn
    their keep: the direct CO₂ effect has been understood since Arrhenius (1896) and is
    not in dispute; water vapour (itself a greenhouse gas whose amount is set by
    temperature — a fast, strong positive feedback, measured by satellites) roughly
    doubles the response; ice–albedo adds more, concentrated at the poles (polar
    amplification, observed); the lapse-rate feedback partly cancels in the tropics;
    and clouds — the wildcard — could add or subtract a couple of tenths each way,
    which is precisely the width of the model spread. Current measured totals: the
    net human forcing is ~2.7 W/m² (after aerosol cooling is subtracted), and Earth's
    energy imbalance — measured independently by satellites and ocean heat content —
    is ~0.9 W/m² and growing, the planet's literal ledger entry.
  </p>

  <h2>Scenarios: the IF the models are given</h2>
  <p>
    Models do not predict choices, so the community writes <strong>scenarios</strong>:
    internally coherent futures for population, technology, energy and policy, run
    through integrated assessment models to produce emissions. The current families are
    the <strong>SSPs</strong> (Shared Socioeconomic Pathways) with radiative-forcing
    labels: <strong>SSP1-1.9</strong> (the most ambitious — net-zero CO₂ around 2050,
    ~1.4–1.5 °C by 2100, roughly the Paris Agreement's stretch path);
    <strong>SSP1-2.6</strong> (~1.8 °C — overshoot then decline);
    <strong>SSP2-4.5</strong> (middle-of-the-road, ~2.7 °C);
    <strong>SSP3-7.0</strong> (regional rivalry, ~3.6 °C); <strong>SSP5-8.5</strong>
    (fossil-fuelled expansion, ~4.4 °C — a doubling-plus of the blanket). Reading
    discipline: the spread between pathways is <em>human-choice uncertainty</em>, the
    spread within one pathway is <em>model uncertainty</em> — conflating them is the
    standard way projections get misread ("models can't agree" when the models agree
    and the futures differ). Two further pieces of professional vocabulary:
    <strong>carbon budgets</strong> — the cumulative tonnes consistent with a
    temperature target (~500 GtCO₂ remained for a 50% shot at 1.5 °C in AR6, spent
    down yearly); and <strong>committed warming</strong> — the near-term further rise
    already locked in by ocean heat lag, even if emissions stopped tonight (~0.1–0.2
    °C more this century on top of trajectory), which distinguishes "stop the
    increase" (possible by net zero) from "undo the increase" (requires removals).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> scenario pathways like SSP5-8.5 are "predictions",
    and disagreements over their plausibility discredit the models. They are
    <em>boundary conditions</em> — standardised inputs chosen to span the plausible
    policy space, updated by Working Group III economics. The physics machinery that
    turns them into temperature is the same in every case; only the IF differs. (A
    subtler 2020s debate — the "hot model problem" — is real but different: some CMIP6
    models show sensitivities above the assessed range, so the IPCC now weights the
    ensemble rather than averaging it blindly.)
  </p>

  <h2>How we know the models work — and where they struggle</h2>
  <p>
    Validation is continuous and multi-layered. <strong>Hindcasts</strong>: models
    driven by known forcings reproduce the observed 20th-century warming, including
    the mid-century flattening from sulfate aerosols — and (the decisive test) they
    do <em>not</em> reproduce it with natural forcings alone. <strong>Real-time
    tests</strong>: the cooling from Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption was predicted and
    observed to match. <strong>Retrospective skill</strong>: a 2020 analysis of models
    published since 1970 found the ones whose projections were testable had
    forecast the observed warming with real skill. <strong>Paleoclimate</strong> (the
    earth-science lesson) supplies out-of-sample checks — the Last Glacial Maximum
    and the mid-Pliocene warm period constrain sensitivity from both directions.
    <strong>Ensembles</strong> (dozens of models, many runs each) turn residual spread
    into a measured probability distribution. The known weaknesses are specific and
    named: <strong>ice-sheet dynamics</strong> (sea-level rise has tracked the upper
    edge of projections as processes below the grid — marine ice-sheet instability —
    bite harder than expected), <strong>convection and clouds</strong> (the
    parameterisation frontier), and <strong>tipping elements</strong> (AMOC, permafrost
    carbon, Amazon dieback) which are handled as risk add-ons rather than smooth
    physics — the next lesson's subject. Model families get updated by such
    surprises; none has been invalidated by one.
  </p>

  <h2>Attribution: running the models backwards</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Detection and attribution</strong> is the formal science of blame. The
    method: build the "fingerprint" of each forcing (greenhouse gases warm the
    troposphere and cool the stratosphere — a vertical pattern no solar forcing
    produces; aerosols cool the north; ozone cools the upper stratosphere), then
    compare the observed climate change against worlds simulated with and without
    each. The conclusion, sustained across model generations and data sets since the
    1995 IPCC report: the observed warming is attributable to human emissions with
    ever-tightening confidence ("unequivocal" in AR6). <strong>Event attribution</strong>
    extends the machinery to single disasters — the World Weather Attribution
    collaborations quantify "how much more likely did climate change make this
    heatwave" by comparing simulations of today's world with a counterfactual
    un-warmed one; the same logic is now entering courts as evidence in climate
    litigation. Attribution is, in effect, the models run as a control experiment —
    the paleoclimate lesson's logic, pointed at the present.
  </p>

  <h2>From globe to region: downscaling and what a local projection means</h2>
  <p>
    Policy happens in river basins and cities, not 100-km cells, so global output is
    <strong>downscaled</strong>: dynamically (a regional model nested inside the GCM at
    10–25 km, inheriting the boundary conditions) or statistically (calibrated
    relationships from the observed fine-scale climate, applied to the coarse
    projection). The professional caution: regional projections inherit global
    uncertainty <em>and</em> add their own — circulation changes (storm tracks,
    monsoons) are less certain than the global mean, and some of the sharpest
    regional consequences (droughts, snowpack, agriculture) depend on circulation
    shifts models handle least well. The trustworthy local signals are the robust
    ones: warming (everywhere, more over land and poles), intensified heavy rain
    (warmer air carries ~7% more vapour per degree), rising sea level, and shifts of
    growing seasons — with the attribution machinery able to state, region by
    region, which observed extremes already carry the fingerprint.
  </p>

  <h2>Net zero: why "stop emitting" is a thermodynamic statement</h2>
  <p>
    The headline result of the coupled carbon-cycle models deserves its own section,
    because it shapes every policy debate: temperature responds to <em>cumulative</em>
    emissions, approximately linearly (the <strong>TCRE</strong> — transient climate
    response to cumulative emissions, ≈0.45 °C per 1,000 GtCO₂), and once net emissions
    reach zero the warming roughly <strong>stops</strong> (the "zero emissions
    commitment" is near zero for decades, as ocean-heat lag and falling aerosols and
    sink uptake offset each other). This is why the Paris targets are stated as
    budgets — a finite total that spending exhausts — rather than as annual rates:
    every tonne emitted is fungible across place and decade, and delay is not
    neutral but arithmetic. The corollaries: overshoot scenarios must pay back in
    <em>negative</em> emissions (removals at gigatonne scale, whose land, energy and
    storage demands the sustainability chapter sizes); and methane's short residence
    time (the geochemical-cycles lesson's τ ≈ 10 years) makes its cuts uniquely fast-
    acting — a lever the CO₂ arithmetic does not provide.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The model hierarchy.</strong> The GCM is the flagship of a fleet: energy
    balance models (one or two boxes — the worked example above is one) build
    intuition and run millions of parameter perturbations; radiative–convective
    columns resolve the vertical physics exactly (the historical route to sensitivity);
    intermediate-complexity models (EMICs) trade resolution for tractability and
    simulate the last glacial cycle end to end; ESMs carry the full coupling; and
    "storm-resolving" km-scale models run short periods without convection
    parameterisations — the clearest window on how the cloud problem might collapse.
    Conclusions are trusted when they appear across the hierarchy, not just in the
    flagship.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Emergent constraints and the sensitivity endgame.</strong> A modern
    technique: find an observable relationship <em>within</em> the model ensemble
    (say, between a measurable cloud property today and each model's eventual
    sensitivity), check it against real observations, and use it to narrow the
    ensemble — several such constraints helped AR6 narrow its ECS range for the
    first time in decades (2.5–4 °C, very likely 2–5, best estimate 3, against the
    1979 Charney report's 1.5–4.5: a four-decade-long argument slowly converging).
    The unresolved tails matter more than the middle: low-sensitivity worlds still
    warm past 2 °C under high emissions, while high-sensitivity tails —
    disproportionately strong cloud feedback — dominate the risk calculus for the
    targets.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Cloud feedback remains the largest single
    uncertainty (high-altitude cirrus versus low marine stratus pull opposite ways;
    field campaigns like EUREC4A were designed to watch the trade cumulus decide).
    The AMOC's future and the permafrost carbon bomb (≈1,500 GtC frozen — roughly
    twice the atmosphere's) are risk questions the models handle poorly. Ice-sheet
    projection has a two-decade deficit to make up. And the computational frontier —
    "digital twin" Earth systems at km-scale, exascale machine-learned physics mixed
    with conservation laws — is the live engineering question: whether the next
    generation of models will get their clouds by brute force, by statistics, or by
    the hybrid.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IPCC AR6 WG1 (2021); Arrhenius (1896); Hausfather et al., <em>Geophysical Research Letters</em> (2020) on model skill; CMIP6 ensemble documentation; Sherwood et al. (2020) on ECS assessment.</p>
</TopicPage>
